From: MacSW maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] glu32: Implement the GLU 1.3 NURBS curves and surfaces.

SOLIDWORKS ends with an "Assertion failed" box of the Wine C++ Runtime
Library (dlls/glu32/glu.c) the first time it redraws a sketch that has a
spline or an ellipse: gluNewNurbsRenderer returned NULL, every other NURBS
function was assert(0), and SOLIDWORKS does not check for NULL.

New dlls/glu32/nurbs.c, plain C:
 * gluNewNurbsRenderer, gluDeleteNurbsRenderer, gluNurbsProperty and
   gluGetNurbsProperty with the GLU 1.3 defaults (GLU_PATH_LENGTH, 50
   pixels, 0.5, GLU_U_STEP and GLU_V_STEP 100, GLU_FILL, culling off,
   GLU_AUTO_LOAD_MATRIX on, GLU_NURBS_RENDERER), gluLoadSamplingMatrices.
 * gluNurbsCallback: GLU_NURBS_ERROR and the tessellator callbacks
   GLU_NURBS_BEGIN/VERTEX/NORMAL/COLOR/TEXTURE_COORD/END and their _DATA
   forms. glu32 does not export gluNurbsCallbackData, so the _DATA forms
   get a NULL user pointer.
 * Curves (gluBeginCurve, gluNurbsCurve, gluEndCurve): GL_MAP1_VERTEX_3,
   GL_MAP1_VERTEX_4 (rational) and the normal, color, index and texture
   maps. Surfaces (gluBeginSurface, gluNurbsSurface, gluEndSurface): the
   same GL_MAP2 types; without a GL_MAP2_NORMAL map the normals are
   computed (renderer: only with GL_AUTO_NORMAL, like evaluators).
   Trimming (gluBeginTrim, gluPwlCurve, NURBS trim curves, gluEndTrim) is
   approximate: grid vertices are kept by the winding number of the
   sampled loops, so a trimmed edge is accurate to about one grid cell.
 * Points come from de Boor's algorithm on each knot span. The steps per
   span come from the sampling method (path length or parametric error in
   pixels through the sampling matrices, the same in object space, or the
   domain distance), at most 1024 per span and 65536 per curve, and 256
   along each direction of a surface, so a tiny tolerance cannot make a
   huge loop. Each span keeps at least one step, so a surface with very
   many knot spans can still need a huge grid or trimming loop: when the
   grid or all trimming loops together would need more than 256 MB, the
   surface gives GLU_OUT_OF_MEMORY. These counts are checked before they
   can overflow and before any memory is allocated for them.
 * The renderer draws with glBegin/glVertex3fv (rational points are
   divided by w), so it goes through the immediate-mode batching of patch
   0030; evaluated colors, normals and texture coordinates do not change
   the current ones (glPushAttrib(GL_CURRENT_BIT)).
 * Invalid input goes to the error callback with GLU_NURBS_ERROR1-37,
   GLU_INVALID_ENUM, GLU_INVALID_VALUE or GLU_OUT_OF_MEMORY and is not
   drawn. Every entry point ignores a NULL object. Nothing asserts.
 * TRACE on every entry point (WINEDEBUG=+glu).

include/wine/glu.h gets the GLU 1.3 NURBS constants. dlls/glu32/tests
check the tessellator callbacks. See docs/glu-nurbs.md.

